this.add({id:3332, 
title:"Configuring the ACL & Route Map", 
body:" configuring acl & route map you use route map web traffic ports 80 443 gre tunnels. to configure access control list (acl) route map:  create acl traffic want match apply route map to: (config)#access-list 10 permit tcp any eq www\n(config)#access-list 20 443 define route map match traffic against: (config)#route-map netskope permit 10 assign access-list route map created step 2: (config-route-map)#match ip address 2000 set tunnel interfaces order priority: (config-route-map)#set interface tunnel1 tunnel2 apply route map interface traffic rerouted from: (config-route-map)#interface gigabitethernet2\n(config-if)#ip policy route-map netskope " });
this.add({id:3333, 
title:"Configuring the Failover Options", 
body:" configuring failover options cisco provides options automate failover primary backup gre tunnels.  you use keepalives tunnel combine ip sla with embedded event manager (eem) failover. netskope recommends using easy configure.  " });
this.add({id:3334, 
title:"Sample CLI Configuration", 
body:" sample cli configuration below sample cli gre tunnel configuration cisco routers: interface gigabitethernet1 \n ip address dhcp\n nat outside\n negotiation auto\n mop enabled\n sysid\n!\ninterface gigabitethernet2\n 10.2.16.139 255.255.255.0\n policy route-map netskope\n tunnel1 \n description \"primary gre tunnel\"\n unnumbered gigabitethernet1\n ip tcp adjust-mss 1436\n keepalive 10 3\n tunnel source destination 66.151.135.64\n!\ninterface tunnel2\n \"backup 163.116.136.185\n!\nip access-list extended 2000\n permit any eq 443\n 20 www\n!\nroute-map netskope 10\n match set tunnel1 " });
